# 8. Stage Four: Mineral Nucleation and Fouling Conditions

Extend the model toward actual mineral deposition, beginning with CaCO3.

Use Santoro et al. (2021) as the primary reference.

Reproduce its heterogeneous-nucleation formulation to the extent that all required equations and membrane parameters are available.

Investigate the relationship between nucleation propensity and:

- Membrane contact angle.
- Membrane surface charge.
- Permittivity.
- Surface roughness.
- Local supersaturation.
- Relevant temperature and chemical conditions.

Calculate nucleation-related quantities only where supported by the selected model.

Do not automatically describe a supersaturated case as an actual fouled case.

Distinguish:

A. Clean membrane:
No deposited mineral is assumed.

B. Scaling-risk condition:
The chemical solution is near or above saturation, but actual deposition is not established.

C. Nucleation-prone condition:
The heterogeneous-nucleation model predicts favorable nucleation conditions under its documented assumptions.

D. Confirmed or modeled deposition:
Only use this category if a physically specified deposition or crystal-growth law and its parameters are available.

### Critical requirement for time-dependent fouling

Investigate whether a suitable RED-specific mineral-deposition growth model exists in the cited literature or can be defensibly derived from validated experimental data.

If a complete, parameterized growth model is available:

- Implement its governing equations.
- Document its calibration data.
- Calculate deposition evolution over time.
- Update the membrane properties consistently.
- Recalculate the affected transport and performance fields.

If no sufficiently supported growth law is available:

- Do not invent deposition-rate coefficients.
- Do not fabricate deposit thickness versus operating time.
- Do not claim to simulate real long-term fouling.
- Keep the output at the level of mineral saturation and nucleation propensity.
- Optionally run separately labeled fixed-state sensitivity scenarios to examine how assumed deposit resistance would affect performance.

Such sensitivity scenarios must be marked as hypothetical rather than physically validated fouling trajectories.

Target approximately 20–30 mineral-scaling-related cases, depending on the capabilities of the verified model.

